> If you repeatedly do something that has a modest chance of
> happening, eventually it becomes likely that it will happen.

Actually no, this is completely wrong. It will not become more likely.

That's the point of theory of probability; if you have a stochastical
process with a certain probability for an event to happen, that event
will happen _with the same given probability each time_ you trigger it.
Only if you consider large numbers of test sequences the observed
results asymptotically approximate the given probability. That means
that you cannot count on a higher success rate, just because you have
already had many "bad luck" events in the past. Each individually
triggered event is uncorrelated to the one triggered before.

That's why so regularly astonishing long streaks of (bad) luck can be
observed in Nethack; I have posted a few of such streaks in the past.

